@meta
id: 04-18
title: The payout export
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: count-prefix, relativenumber, hjkl, gj-gk
par: 130
minutes: 10
vscode: three tabs, a lot of scrolling and two paste mistakes
open: payouts.csv release-notes.md errors.json
vimrc: set relativenumber number

@brief
The last one in the chapter, and the only rule is that no key you press
may be an arrow key. Three files, opened as three buffers in the order
below. `:wn` — write, next — saves the file you are in and moves you to
the following one. `:wq` on the last file finishes the job, and `:q`
leaves a file untouched. Proper file and buffer juggling is chapters 16
and 18; those three are all you need today.

**payouts.csv** — forty-eight lines. One row lost its currency column in
the export: it is the only line with four fields instead of five. Put
`,GBP` back on the end of it. Then add four identical reconciliation rows
to the bottom of the file, each exactly:

    PAY-0000,MER-00,0,PENDING,GBP

**release-notes.md** — two additions. Under `## Unreleased`, below the
existing bullet, add:

    - record the currency on every payout row

And the top heading, `# Payouts service`, needs an underline directly
beneath it: a line of `=` exactly as long as the heading text, which is
seventeen characters.

**errors.json** — nothing to do. It is open so you can check
`PAYOUT_CURRENCY_MISSING` exists. Change a single byte of it and this
lesson fails.

@keys
| `26j` | down twenty-six lines |
| `4o` | open four lines, repeating what you type on each |
| `17i=` `Esc` | insert seventeen equals signs |
| `:wn` | write this file and move to the next one |
| `:q` | leave this one untouched — it is the last file, so this exits |

@hints
Work the CSV top to bottom: the broken row first, then the last line, then
the four new rows. That way you only read the margin twice and both
numbers point downwards.
--
For the release notes, do the bullet first and the underline second, or
you will read a margin number that your own new line has already made
wrong.
--
The underline is `o` `Esc` to make an empty line under the heading, then
`17i=` and `Esc`. The four CSV rows are `4o`, the row typed once, `Esc`.
--
End with `:wn` on the CSV, `:wn` on the release notes, and `:q` on
errors.json. If you accidentally typed into errors.json, `u` until the
file is clean, then `:q`.

@solution
    payouts.csv     26j A ,GBP <Esc>
                    21j 4o PAY-0000,MER-00,0,PENDING,GBP <Esc> :wn<Enter>

    release-notes   4j o - record the currency on every payout row <Esc>
                    5k o <Esc> 17i= <Esc> :wn<Enter>

    errors.json     :q<Enter>

Around a hundred and ten keystrokes for three files, and roughly
eighty of those are text that had to be typed by somebody.

Look back at what you have actually got out of this chapter. Four keys
that never leave the home row. A number in front of any of them that says
exactly how far. A margin that hands you that number so you never do the
arithmetic. A count on `o` and `i` that writes repeated text once. And
the judgement to notice when the count is getting silly and something
else is called for.

That is a small set of ideas, and it is the foundation for everything
from here on, because the count generalises. In chapter 5, `3w` moves
three words. In chapter 9, `d3j` deletes four lines. In chapter 21, `10@q`
runs a macro ten times. You learn the counting once.

What you should be left with is a habit rather than a key: before you
move, know where you are going. Arrow keys and scrolling are the opposite
habit — move a bit, look, move a bit more, look again. The reason this
chapter kept putting the target twenty-six lines away was to make that
difference impossible to miss.

